WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
It's a cold and windy winter solstice, and I'm in one of the best places in the world to celebrate it

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Avebury henge, in Wiltshire.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.920
It's basically Stonehenge's lesser known cousin. And I reckon it's better.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.860
If you believe the new agers, then Avebury, this whole site-

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:19.340
It's enourmous by the way, it's many times the size of Stonehenge. Enourmous earthworks, three stone circles.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.380
This is the center of a swirl of energy highways, Ley Lines as they're known.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.500
In the 1920's a man called Alfred Watkins brought out a book called "The Old Straight Track".

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.260
The idea that there were dead straight tracks running across Britain that ancient peoples would follow.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.780
In the 1970's that got mixed up with a whole load of "New Age" beliefs.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:40.500
By someone called John Michell.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:47.180
He came up with the idea of Ley Lines. Mystical energy highways that spread across Britain, and presumably the world.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.820
Thing is, if you look at the data, if you look at where the Ley Lines actually are, you can see the patterns.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.900
You can see points in a line, and the Ley Lines following them.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.000
Well... you can.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.780
But also if you just put a hundred random dots down on a map, mathematically you can also draw lines between them.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.980
You can also find Ley Lines between Woolworths stores, as mathematician Matt Parker did.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:15.980
Or between old red telephone boxes. Since Britain has thousands and thousands of scheduled ancient monuments.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.880
But, well this is still a site of great spiritual significance.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.560
As I've wandered around there've been a lot of new age pagans around.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.140
Some drumming, some just smoking pot, and a couple just kinda putting their heads to the stones.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:32.920
This is why this place is better than Stonehenge. Stonehenge, you can't get close to the stones, except on special days.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.840
You certainly can never touch them.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.400
But here, well

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.400
These stones have been torn down and put back up a couple of times by various branches of Christianity and paganism that've come through.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.360
Someone has build a villiage in the middle of the standing stones and run a road through it.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.220
There are some that've just been replaced with small markers to show where evidence says a stone once was.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.560
No one really complains that the Sistine Chapel is restored every now and then.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.420
I don't think we should complain that these stones have been restored either.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:07.000
This is a very real way of having some kind of connection to the ancient humans who were here

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.840
four and a half, maybe five thousand years ago, and

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.680
who put these stones up for...

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.540
Well. We'll never know what reason

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.760
and if that means we get a little bit of
new age beliefs...

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.800
Well, I can't really begrudge that.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.480
Happy winter solstice.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.760
